permalink: /tools/discount-calculator/
h1: "Discount Calculator"
extraCss: "/tools/discount-calculator/style.css"
howto:
  name: "How to Calculate Sale Price and Discount Savings"
  description: "Step-by-step instructions for calculating retail discounts, stacked coupons, and sales tax."
  steps:
    - name: "Select Currency & Original Price"
      text: "Choose your currency symbol and enter the item's original sticker price."
    - name: "Enter Primary Discount Rate"
      text: "Input the advertised sale discount percentage (e.g. 20%)."
    - name: "Add Stacked Coupons or Tax (Optional)"
      text: "Optionally add an extra promo code discount and local sales tax rate."
    - name: "Review Final Cost & Total Savings"
      text: "View your final out-of-pocket price, total money saved, and the step-by-step math breakdown."
faqs:
  - q: "How do you calculate the final discounted price of an item?"
    a: "Multiply the original sticker price by the discount percentage divided by 100 to find total savings, then subtract the savings from the original price to determine the final sale price."
  - q: "Why does a 20% sale plus a 10% coupon not equal 30% total discount?"
    a: "Because stacked store coupons apply to the already reduced sale subtotal rather than the original sticker price. A $100 item reduced by 20% becomes $80; applying 10% off $80 saves $8, bringing the final price to $72 (28% overall savings, not 30%)."
  - q: "Is sales tax calculated before or after applying discounts?"
    a: "In retail store purchases, sales tax is calculated on the net discounted subtotal after all promotional store discounts and coupons have been applied."
  - q: "Can I calculate stacked double discounts with this tool?"
    a: "Yes. Enter your primary sale discount percentage in the first field and your secondary store coupon percentage in the optional stacked discount field."
  - q: "Is any financial data transmitted to a server?"
    a: "No. All discount calculations execute locally inside your web browser using JavaScript. No price numbers or financial inputs leave your device."
sources:
  - label: "Federal Trade Commission - Pricing & Discounts Guidance"
    url: "https://www.ftc.gov/"
  - label: "NIST Consumer Pricing Mathematics Standards"
    url: "https://www.nist.gov/"
  - label: "ISO 80000-2 Mathematical signs and symbols in physical sciences and technology"
    url: "https://www.iso.org/standard/64973.html"
